<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-strong-sheafiness">
  <title>Strong sheafiness</title>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-sheafy">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The ring <m>A</m> is strongly sheafy.  In particular, the Huber pair
<m>(A,A^\circ)</m> is sheafy.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr">Lean (isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="870d0eed2c48a020109d766d2af89c3f47469a94" ref="FiniteJet.finiteJet_tateExt_isSheafyComplete">Lean (finiteJet_tateExt_isSheafyComplete)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Give <m>B,C,D</m> their maximal rings of integral elements.  The max/Gauss estimates <xref ref="eq-structural-map-bounds"/> show that every structural map is norm-nonincreasing.  Consequently it sends bounded subsets to bounded subsets; in particular, if <m>x</m> is power-bounded, then the set of powers of its image is the image of the bounded set <m>\{x^n:n\geq0\}</m>.  The structural maps therefore send power-bounded elements to power-bounded elements and define morphisms of Huber pairs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each of <m>B,C,D</m> is a classical strongly noetherian affinoid <m>k</m>-algebra and
is therefore sheafy by Huber's theorem <xref ref="bib-Huber94" detail="Theorem 2.2"/>.  Let
<m>U=\bigcup_iU_i</m> in <m>\Spa(A,A^\circ)</m>.  Push the datum for <m>U</m> and the data
for the <m>U_i</m> to <m>B,C,D</m>.  If a section over <m>U</m> restricts to zero on every
<m>U_i</m>, its images over <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> vanish by separatedness there.
Injectivity of the localised Milnor row from
<xref ref="prop-localized-milnor"/> then shows that the original section is zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now take a matching family over the <m>U_i</m>.  Its images glue over <m>B</m> and
<m>C</m>.  The two glued sections have the same image over <m>D</m>, since their
restrictions agree on the pushed cover and the presheaf for <m>D</m> is
separated.  Exactness of the localised Milnor row over <m>U</m> gives a section
over <m>A</m>, and injectivity of the corresponding rows over the <m>U_i</m> shows
that it restricts to the given family.
      </p>
      <p>
        We must also check the topological part of the sheaf condition.  The image
of
<me>\mathcal O(U)\longrightarrow\prod_i\mathcal O(U_i)</me>
is exactly the subspace of tuples whose two restrictions to every
<m>U_i\cap U_j</m> agree.  This subspace is closed: it is the common zero locus
of the continuous difference maps to <m>\mathcal O(U_i\cap U_j)</m>, and hence is
a Banach <m>k</m>-space.  The restriction map is a continuous bijection from the
Banach <m>k</m>-space <m>\mathcal O(U)</m> to this closed subspace.  The
nonarchimedean open mapping theorem therefore makes it a homeomorphism.
This proves the sheaf condition on finite rational covers.  The
sheaf-on-a-rational-basis theorem <xref ref="bib-Stacks" detail="Tag 009O"/> then gives the
all-open topological sheaf condition for <m>(A,A^\circ)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same proof applies after adjoining <m>V_1,\ldots,V_s</m>.  The split Milnor
row extends coefficientwise, the three other vertices remain strongly
noetherian affinoid <m>k</m>-algebras, and the Koszul argument above is unchanged.
Thus <m>A\langle V_1,\ldots,V_s\rangle</m> is sheafy for every <m>s\geq0</m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <proof>
    <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-main"/></title>
    <p>
      <xref ref="prop-uniform-domain"/> proves that <m>A</m> is complete, uniform,
non-noetherian, and a domain.  Strong sheafiness is <xref ref="thm-sheafy"/>, and
failure of stable uniformity is <xref ref="prop-not-stably-uniform"/>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>
